WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.565
Array bounds safety is not a single feature.

2
00:00:03.565 --> 00:00:09.804
It is a disciplined engineering habit across design, implementation, testing, review, build, and release.

3
00:00:09.804 --> 00:00:17.381
The roadmap begins by preserving the information that makes access valid: size, range, ownership, mutability, and lifetime.

4
00:00:17.381 --> 00:00:27.631
Prefer containers, bounded views, and range-aware access when they fit the design. std::array, std::vector, std::string, std::span, iterator ranges,

5
00:00:27.631 --> 00:00:33.424
and standard algorithms can reduce manual tracking and make intent easier to review.

6
00:00:33.424 --> 00:00:37.881
Raw pointer interfaces should be narrow, justified, and clearly documented.

7
00:00:37.881 --> 00:00:39.218
Validate before access.

8
00:00:39.218 --> 00:00:49.022
Indexes, offsets, lengths, counts, and subranges should be checked as complete range decisions, especially when they come from external input or are

9
00:00:49.022 --> 00:00:50.805
derived from multiple fields.

10
00:00:50.805 --> 00:00:56.153
A valid start value does not prove the full range is valid.

11
00:00:56.153 --> 00:00:57.935
Improve legacy code incrementally.

12
00:00:57.935 --> 00:01:05.066
Prioritize code that handles external formats, crosses trust boundaries, manages memory, or mixes offsets and lengths.

13
00:01:05.066 --> 00:01:11.751
Add regression tests, wrap unsafe interfaces, replace unclear range logic, and document assumptions that remain.

14
00:01:11.751 --> 00:01:15.316
Finally, reinforce the process with tools and governance.

15
00:01:15.316 --> 00:01:23.338
Boundary tests, sanitizers, compiler diagnostics, hardened library modes, release verification, and code review all make defects visible earlier.

16
00:01:23.338 --> 00:01:28.240
Revisit exceptions as the codebase, compiler, platform, and team practices evolve.
